  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p><b>Set up a parallel Elastic Beanstalk environment and use the Swap Environment URLs feature</b> is the preferred deployment method for minimizing downtime during application rollbacks. With this approach, you duplicate your existing (production) environment and deploy the new version to the parallel environment. You can thoroughly test the new deployment, and if the upgrade passes validation, the <b>Swap Environment URLs</b> capability allows you to instantly redirect users to the updated environment with virtually zero downtime. If a rollback is needed, you just swap back to the original environment, again with minimal interruption.<br><br><i>Implement Rolling Updates within Elastic Beanstalk, which will automatically revert to the previous version if any update fails</i> is incorrect because while Rolling Updates can reduce downtime and offer basic rollback function if failures are detected, they do not guarantee a near-instant rollback for all types of potential failures—especially when issues only surface after deployment completes.<br><br><i>Develop an AWS CloudFormation template to duplicate your Beanstalk resources, and manually deploy this if needed to control rollbacks</i> is not ideal for urgent rollback needs as creating and deploying resources via CloudFormation is typically slower, more manual, and subject to greater risk of configuration drift or error.<br><br><i>Launch a duplicate Elastic Beanstalk environment and associate a separate Route 53 DNS name with it, making the new domain available to users after deployment</i> is less efficient because users must be informed or redirected manually to the new DNS name. This process introduces more potential for user confusion or downtime compared to the seamless Swap Environment URLs approach.<br><br><strong>Tip:</strong> Using parallel environments with the Swap URL feature is a best practice for AWS Beanstalk zero-downtime deployments and reliable, fast rollback with critical workloads.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your company hosts a mission-critical application on AWS Elastic Beanstalk in its production environment.<br>You are planning to release updates to this application and must guarantee that if a rollback is necessary, it will be performed with minimal downtime for end users.<br><br>Which of the following strategies would best ensure rapid rollback with the least service interruption?</p>",
